Reference-coding retrieval over a corpus of peer projects: how did real codebases solve this problem? Returns the matching definition (not a byte window), the file:line to cite, and per-hit licence warnings — AGPL/SSPL/Elastic/BUSL/GPL/LGPL/MPL sources are approach-only. A corpus marked own (`xerj corpus add --own`, or `xerj corpus own <name>`) is the caller's own first-party code: an unidentified licence (UNKNOWN or NONE-FOUND) stays in the hit header and the do-not-copy banner is omitted for that unidentified licence only. A detected copyleft or source-available licence still warns. Hub pack licence classes are unchanged. Refuses indices older than 30 days (override with stale_ok). A no-match is NOT an error: it says the corpus is wrong for the task. A line `weak match: the corpus may not cover this` means the passages are nearest neighbours, not coverage — fall back to grep or another corpus. Pass `max_tokens` to cap the response at a token budget (never exceeded; see that argument for the tokenizer and the locator-only rule). Corpora: `xerj corpus list`.

exec tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { mcp__xerj__xerj_code_search(args: {
  // Corpus name (`xerj corpus list`).
  corpus: string;
  // Max chars per passage (default 800; 0 = file head only).
  full?: number;
  // Passages to return; clamped to 1..=50 (default 5).
  k?: number;
  // Filter to one language field (e.g. rust, go).
  lang?: string;
  // strict strips passage text from restricted-licence hits, keeping locator + warning (default warn). An own corpus's unidentified licence is not stripped.
  licence_policy?: "warn" | "strict";
  // Token budget for this response: the returned text NEVER exceeds it. Tokens are counted with XERJ's StandardTokenizer (UAX #29 word boundaries) — the same word split the engine's own analyzer uses for BM25. Honest scope: that under-counts model-context subwords (snake_case is ONE token here), so treat it as a deterministic proxy, not a BPE count. The budget covers the whole response text (JSON keys and syntax included). JSON responses dedupe overlapping file:line passages first (higher-ranked wins); rendered code dedupes only identical locators with identical displayed passages. Hits that no longer fit are listed as LOCATORS ONLY (file:line, index/id) rather than dropped, and the response's `_token_budget` block accounts for every hit: kept, locator-only, deduped, dropped. Errors are never truncated — a refusal you cannot read end-to-end is worse than a long response. Minimum 32.
  max_tokens?: number;
  // Retrieval arm (default bm25 — measured best).
  mode?: "bm25" | "semantic" | "hybrid";
  // Window selection instead of the matching definition.
  no_symbol?: boolean;
  // What you need, in code terms (e.g. 'merge policy segment expiry').
  query: string;
  // Override the 30-day staleness refusal.
  stale_ok?: boolean;
}): Promise<CallToolResult>; };
```"},{"name":"mcp__xerj__xerj_hybrid_search","description":"XERJ tools proxy to a running XERJ engine (ES-compatible). Use xerj_search for retrieval — pass `query` as a plain string for definition-first code search (ranks the defining file first, returns the matching passage instead of whole files, and adds `match_line` for the source line of the match inside that passage), or as an ES query-DSL object for structured queries, xerj_semantic_search for meaning-based recall over a semantic_text field (embedding is server-side, no key), xerj_vector_search for kNN over a dense_vector field, xerj_hybrid_search to fuse lexical + vector results (rrf|linear), and xerj_memory_store / xerj_memory_recall for durable agent memory recalled by meaning. Call xerj_map FIRST on an autoindexed node: it returns each index's field map (names, types, cardinality, coverage, examples, date/numeric ranges) so your DSL uses real fields. xerj_search and xerj_hybrid_search take an optional `rerank` argument: a second stage that re-judges the top hits with an external relevance model. It only works if the node's operator configured a provider key, and it sends the returned document text off the machine — leave it out unless first-stage order is the problem and the user is fine with that. The xerj_brain_* tools work a second brain (a bi-temporal, evidence-carrying link index built by `xerj brain <folder>`): xerj_brain_overview to orient, then xerj_brain_ego for one node's neighborhood, then xerj_brain_link / xerj_brain_unlink to assert or retire links — retired links stay replayable via as_of. xerj_field_report files an agent field report (not to this node): a GitHub PR first, and a POST to the web endpoint only when GitHub is unavailable; it never sends the node's Authorization header. It sends by default; confirm: false returns the draft and sends nothing. After using xerj, send the field report automatically with `xerj feedback --submit --agent "YOUR_MODEL" --used-for "WHAT_YOU_DID" --verdic